WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.550
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Claudia Goldin, who's a professor of

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:09.110
economics at Harvard University and author
of the new book Career and Family, Women's

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:12.430
Sensually, Long Journey Toward Equity.

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:13.820
It's really great to have you on today.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.010
I appreciate your time.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.430
Speaker 5: I'm pleased to be here.

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:23.560
Speaker 1: So I think maybe to start our conversation,
it might be interesting as you catalog in

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:30.269
the book to talk about some of the catalysts
over the last 100 years that have led women

00:00:30.269 --> 00:00:35.260
to enter the workforce in greater numbers
now than at any previous time.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:39.620
Can you talk a little bit about kind of the
main highlights on these last hundred years?

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.420
Speaker 5: It's certainly so.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.020
To begin with.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.969
In fact, the most important change in the
labor force in almost every country in the

00:00:46.969 --> 00:00:51.950
world has been the increase of women in the
workforce.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:59.559
So, so this decision is sort of made by considering
what we call the opportunity cost of being

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.710
in the household.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:06.920
So when paid work is more remunerative, it
it's worth more relative to home production.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.789
You exit that household and you go to work,
you know, and this can come about because

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:14.590
real wages increase.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.991
It can also come about because household production
is better alternatives.

00:01:17.991 --> 00:01:24.049
All you have to think about is what it would
be like to bake bread yourself rather than

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:25.220
buying that.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:29.560
Of course, lots of people bake bread and they
do a very good job.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:35.310
But and it might also be furthered when social
norms and traditions change.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:38.479
So you asked about the catalysts.

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:43.079
So technological change have lots of different
types, did it?

00:01:43.079 --> 00:01:48.459
So the most important ones are the ones that
decreased the importance of what we call Brawne

00:01:48.459 --> 00:01:49.459
work.

00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:50.700
Now I'm really strong.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.819
I'm strong into my husband and I know, but
there are a lot of men who is stronger than

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:58.700
I am, and it increased the value of brain
work.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.169
That's really very important.

00:02:00.169 --> 00:02:06.659
So and then there are the technologies that
made homes cleaner and their care less labor

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:07.659
intensive.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:12.640
You know, we forget about the fact we take
for granted that we have clean water and sewage

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.640
removal.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.290
We have flush toilets and central heating,
then household electricity.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:23.349
And finally, there are those that enabled
women to control their fertility is really

00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:25.730
very, very important.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:32.900
And that led to an enormously large increase
in the age of first marriage and as late as

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:40.849
the 1970s, when the birth control pill finally
diffused among among young women.

00:02:40.849 --> 00:02:45.730
So those are the main ones
Speaker 1: you have spoken about in the new

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:46.730
book.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:52.849
You talk about the significantly increased
rate of women who get college degrees and

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:56.780
at a very top level, we know that at least
right now, this may change.

00:02:56.780 --> 00:03:01.260
But at least right now, people with college
degrees have higher lifetime earnings.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.260
Can you talk a little bit about the educational
component to this as well?

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:07.760
Speaker 5: Sure.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:16.489
So I think that you're asking why it is that
women versus men get more degrees, so.

00:03:16.489 --> 00:03:21.189
So the returns are extremely high on the margin.

00:03:21.189 --> 00:03:27.519
All these, you know, the vast majority of
individuals who don't get a college degree

00:03:27.519 --> 00:03:29.969
would do much better if they if they did.

00:03:29.969 --> 00:03:32.690
That's not true for everyone, but it's true
on the margin.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:38.319
But first, a few important facts that are
generally not known.

00:03:38.319 --> 00:03:46.420
There have been more female college graduates
in the US than male college graduates since

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:48.450
around 1980.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:50.920
So therefore, it didn't happen yesterday.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.930
It didn't even happen ten years ago, began,
believe it or not, 40 years ago.

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:01.280
The second factor of importance is that one
high school graduation was a really important

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:08.989
milestone and when just 10 to 20 percent of
the nation's young people would cross that

00:04:08.989 --> 00:04:09.989
threshold.

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:16.980
And I mean, like in the first quarter century
of the the first quarter of the 20th century,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.900
women were the vast majority of high school
graduates.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:23.030
That's an important fact.

00:04:23.030 --> 00:04:28.060
And the third fact is that there are more
female than male college graduates and virtually

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:35.169
all the rich countries in the world and even
in the poorer countries, women are increasing

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:37.550
their numbers relative to men.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.850
So we need to understand what's going on.

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.849
We have to take all of this into consideration.

00:04:43.849 --> 00:04:47.289
So two facts or two notions.

00:04:47.289 --> 00:04:54.800
The first is that work for men is still partially
brawn driven so guys can still do construction

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:55.800
work.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:56.800
Go to any construction site.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.030
How many women do you see?

00:04:58.030 --> 00:05:00.440
Very, very few.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:06.759
And so men, even without a college degree,
can still find work that we used to call good

00:05:06.759 --> 00:05:07.759
work.

00:05:07.759 --> 00:05:12.470
It's become less and less good, although the
demand for non-college graduates today is

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:13.470
very hard.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:19.690
And the second point is that men are let's
call them males because most of them are very

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:23.910
young, don't get their act together until
later in life.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:29.810
And this is reflected in just a host of risky
behaviors, from drugs to crime to those that

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.319
result in their own bodily harm.

00:05:32.319 --> 00:05:37.490
And so those who study psychology and genetics
can point to some of the underlying reasons,

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:42.610
and it means that the boys don't write their
college essays on time and they don't apply

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.949
on time and they don't go when we.

00:05:45.949 --> 00:05:52.280
Speaker 1: Talk about this issue with most
Americans who are sort of centered in the

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:54.770
political debates of our time.

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:59.570
A lot of the factors that you're talking about
to build up this foundation of our understanding

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:06.030
of the dynamics of the workforce often get
sort of tested by the final output, which

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:11.120
is what does pay look like and end questions
of pay disparity.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:18.030
And for a very long time, this idea of the
77 cents on the dollar was being floated about

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:19.410
about eight years ago.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:24.169
We did a kind of deeper dove into this and
and some of your research was actually part

00:06:24.169 --> 00:06:28.919
of of what we used to figure out what are
maybe more accurate numbers.

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:33.349
And it seems that the 77 cent number is simply
not an accurate number from what I'm recalling

00:06:33.349 --> 00:06:35.020
now from the research.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.250
It seemed to be closer to 92 cents on the
dollar.

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.900
Some part of which might be due to discrimination.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.040
Can you talk a little bit about that?

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.040
OK.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:47.040
OK.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.150
I know this is a huge can of worms.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.419
I understand.

00:06:49.419 --> 00:06:55.650
Speaker 5: As someone once said, a can of
worms larger than the universe, which is a

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.460
little scary.

00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.790
So the first thing is that.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:04.120
First of all, Tan, 77, is 82, I believe.

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.300
But it is an accurate number for something.

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:08.430
Everything's an accurate number.

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:14.410
If I told you the price of apples, you know,
in the corner store, that would be an accurate

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:19.520
number for the price of apples at the corner
store may not be the correct number for the

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.690
price of apples in San Diego or Omaha.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:26.550
So that that's part of the issue.

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:35.430
So it is constructed by taking everyone in
the current population survey, taking their

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:43.669
weekly earnings for men and women, and using
only those who are full time year round.

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:51.069
So that that sort of standardizes and some
men are not entirely and then takes the median

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:54.039
for women and the median for men and divides.

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:55.560
That's a no.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:58.009
It's an accurate number for what it's doing.

00:07:58.009 --> 00:08:00.680
OK, here's the inaccuracies.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:11.389
The inaccuracies are that for each woman,
the amount that she receives relative to men

00:08:11.389 --> 00:08:13.210
changes over her lifetime.

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:20.129
We know that we know that for relatively young
men and women, their earnings are pretty similar.

00:08:20.129 --> 00:08:28.190
You know, if I look at what happens to college
graduates who are law school graduates when

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:31.969
they first leave, their earnings are very
similar.

00:08:31.969 --> 00:08:36.830
And then over time, they diverge and they
diverge a tremendous amount.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:42.270
For those at the highest and the professionals,
so lawyers.

00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:49.440
So to lawyers, my boss both go to a Park Avenue
law firm and then they marry.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.950
They have kids and they realize it's unsustainable.

00:08:53.950 --> 00:09:00.790
And so what happens is that it's often the
case that the woman will remain a lawyer but

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:06.160
move to a firm that smaller and less demanding,
less greedy.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:12.200
The difference between their earnings that
ratio isn't 77, it's not 92.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:20.230
It's often sixty four, you know, but we generally
don't observe in census data where we're since

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:24.600
we're narrowing this, we're looking at the
medians.

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:27.890
These are the very, very upper tail.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:33.760
If we looked at the lower tail, then they're
going to be far more similar.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:39.850
It's almost ironic have often been asked,
isn't the gender gap wider at the bottom?

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:43.230
I said, I'm going to be wider at the bottom.

00:09:43.230 --> 00:09:50.080
We've got a we've got a bottom that's limited
by the minimum wage.

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.500
So it's not as if you have so much room at
the bottom, you have a tremendous amount of

00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:57.330
room at the top.

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.650
And so that's that's the issue.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:05.250
So it is a correct number, but it's not a
number that's going to tell us a lot about

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:12.740
